(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["IoT & Cyber-Physical Systems"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["IoT & Cyber-Physical Systems"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# IoT and Cyber-Physical Systems: Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nShow formulas, substitute numbers and give units. All numerical answers below were computed in Python (path-loss and LoRa airtime formulas, battery arithmetic, filters, PID simulation). Where a datasheet value is missing state your assumption.\n\n# Assignment 1: Sensing, Conversion, Buses and Power (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. ADC resolution (10 marks)** (a) Find the LSB size of a 14-bit ADC with a 2.5 V reference and of a 12-bit ADC with a 3.3 V reference [3]. (b) A sensor outputs 10 mV/°C. What temperature step corresponds to one LSB in each case? [2] (c) What code does the 14-bit converter give for 1.0 V? [2] (d) Give the ideal SNR of the 14-bit ADC [1] and say how averaging 16 samples helps [2].\n\n**Q2. Thermistor measurement (14 marks)** A 10 kΩ NTC (B = 3435 K, R₀ = 10 kΩ at 25 °C) is connected from 3.3 V to the ADC pin, with a fixed 10 kΩ resistor from the ADC pin to ground. The ADC is 12 bits with a 3.3 V reference. (a) Derive R_NTC from the ADC code [3]. (b) Find the temperature for codes 1800 and 2600 [6]. (c) Compute the resistance at 0 °C and 85 °C [3]. (d) Estimate the self-heating power at 25 °C and say why it matters [2].\n\n**Q3. Aliasing (8 marks)** (a) A 900 Hz tone is sampled at 1 kHz. At what frequency does it appear? [2] (b) Repeat for 1300 Hz at 1 kHz and 2500 Hz at 4 kHz [3] (c) How do you prevent aliasing in a vibration sensor whose signal of interest reaches 200 Hz? [3]\n\n**Q4. Battery life (16 marks)** A sensor node uses a 2000 mAh battery. It wakes every 5 minutes for 1.2 s at 25 mA and sleeps at 8 µA. (a) Compute the average current and the ideal battery life in years [4]. (b) Recompute if reporting every 30 minutes [3]. (c) Recompute for the 5-minute node if the active time is halved to 0.6 s [3]. (d) If the sleep current is cut to 2 µA instead, what is the life? Which optimization is better and why? [3] (e) Only 70 percent of the nominal capacity is usable at low temperature; recompute (a) [3].\n\n**Q5. Buses (12 marks)** (a) An I²C sensor read is START, address+W, register address, repeated START, address+R, then 3 data bytes (each byte plus ACK is 9 clocks). How many SCL clocks, and how long at 100 kHz, 400 kHz and 1 MHz? [4] (b) A UART at 115,200 baud 8N1 sends 256 bytes. How long does it take, and how long at 9600 baud? [3] (c) A 24-bit SPI ADC conversion is read with a 1 MHz clock. How long is one read and what maximum sample rate follows? [2] (d) On an I²C bus with 3.3 kΩ pull-ups and 200 pF capacitance estimate the rise time. For a 400 kHz bus with 100 pF the rise-time limit is 300 ns: what is the largest pull-up resistance? [3]\n\n**Q6. Solar supply (10 marks)** The node of Q4(a) runs from 3.3 V and you want to power it from a small solar panel with 4 equivalent full-sun hours per day and 60 percent end-to-end efficiency. (a) Compute the average power and daily energy [4]. (b) Compute the panel power required [4]. (c) Name two further considerations when sizing the storage [2].\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) 14-bit, 2.5 V: 2.5/16384 = **0.1526 mV**. 12-bit, 3.3 V: 3.3/4096 = **0.8057 mV**.\n(b) 0.1526 mV ÷ 10 mV/°C = **0.0153 °C**; 0.8057 mV ÷ 10 mV/°C = **0.0806 °C**.\n(c) 1.0/2.5 × 16384 = **6554** (6553.6, rounded).\n(d) Step 1: SNR = 6.02 × 14 + 1.76 = **86.0 dB**.\nStep 2: Averaging N independent noisy samples cuts the random noise by √N: 16 samples give a 4× reduction (and about 2 extra bits of effective resolution if the noise is large enough to dither the LSB).\nStep 3: It does not remove drift or systematic error.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) V = code × 3.3/4096. With the NTC on the top and 10 kΩ at the bottom, V = 3.3 × 10k/(R + 10k), so R = 10k × (3.3 - V)/V.\n(b) Code 1800: V = 1.450 V, R = 10k × 1.850/1.450 = 12.76 kΩ; T = 1/(1/298.15 + ln(1.2756)/3435) = **18.8 °C**. Code 2600: V = 2.095 V, R = 10k × 1.205/2.095 = 5.754 kΩ; T = **40.0 °C**. (A larger code means a higher voltage across the fixed resistor, so a lower NTC resistance and a warmer temperature.)\n(c) R(0 °C) = 10k exp[3435(1/273.15 - 1/298.15)] = **28.7 kΩ**; R(85 °C) = **1.45 kΩ**.\n(d) At 25 °C the divider current is 3.3/20 kΩ = 0.165 mA, so P = I²R = (0.165 mA)² × 10 kΩ = **272 µW**. This heats the bead above ambient (error depends on its dissipation constant, typically 1 to 2 mW/°C, giving 0.1 to 0.3 °C); reduce it by powering the divider only during measurement or using a higher resistance and a larger series resistor.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) The alias is |f - k f_s|: |900 - 1000| = **100 Hz**.\n(b) 1300 Hz at 1 kHz: |1300 - 1000| = **300 Hz**. 2500 Hz at 4 kHz: |2500 - 4000| = **1500 Hz**.\n(c) Add an analog anti-alias low-pass filter with a cutoff just above 200 Hz and sample well above 2 × 200 Hz (for example 1 kHz or more); choose the filter so that its attenuation at f_s/2 is below the ADC's noise level, or oversample and filter digitally.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) I_avg = (25 mA × 1.2 s + 0.008 mA × 298.8 s)/300 s = (30 + 2.39)/300 = **0.108 mA**. Life = 2000/0.108 = 18,520 h = 772 days = **2.11 years**.\n(b) T = 1800 s: I_avg = (30 + 0.008 × 1798.8)/1800 = (30 + 14.4)/1800 = **0.0247 mA** → life 81,100 h = **9.26 years** (self-discharge now matters).\n(c) Active 0.6 s: I_avg = (15 + 0.008 × 299.4)/300 = **0.058 mA** → **3.94 years**.\n(d) Sleep 2 µA: I_avg = (30 + 0.002 × 298.8)/300 = 0.102 mA → **2.24 years**, only a 6 percent gain. Halving the active time almost doubled the life (3.94 years), so **shortening the active burst** (faster start-up, fewer retries, lower TX time) is far more valuable because the active charge (30 mA·s per cycle) dominates the sleep charge (2.4 mA·s).\n(e) Usable capacity 1400 mAh: 1400/0.108 = **1.48 years**. Always derate for temperature, self-discharge, pulse current and ageing.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Clocks: address+W 9 + register 9 + address+R 9 + 3 data bytes 27 = **54 clocks** (START conditions add small extra time). At 100 kHz: 540 µs; at 400 kHz: **135 µs**; at 1 MHz: **54 µs**.\n(b) 256 × 10 bits = 2560 bits: at 115,200 baud **22.2 ms**; at 9600 baud **267 ms**.\n(c) 24 clocks at 1 MHz = **24 µs**; maximum rate 1/24 µs ≈ **41.7 kSa/s** (less if the ADC needs conversion time or CS gaps).\n(d) Step 1: t_r ≈ 0.8473 R C = 0.8473 × 3.3 kΩ × 200 pF = **559 ns** (fine for standard mode, 1000 ns, but too slow for fast mode's 300 ns).\nStep 2: R_max = 300 ns/(0.8473 × 100 pF) = **3.54 kΩ**.\nStep 3: Also check R_min = (V_dd - 0.4 V)/3 mA = 967 Ω for 3.3 V.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Average current 0.108 mA × 3.3 V = **0.356 mW**; daily energy 0.356 mW × 24 h = **8.55 mWh** (about 2.6 mAh at 3.3 V).\n(b) P_panel = 8.55 mWh/(4 h × 0.6) = **3.56 mW** peak panel rating (a tiny panel).\n(c) Storage must carry the node through nights and cloudy spells (sized for several days of autonomy); also temperature limits of the battery or supercapacitor, charge-controller quiescent current, cell self-discharge, panel orientation and shading, and the start-up (cold-boot) behavior after full discharge.\n\n# Assignment 2: Wireless Links, Protocols and Data (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Link budget (14 marks)** A sub-GHz sensor transmits at 10 dBm with a 0 dBi antenna, and the gateway has a 3 dBi antenna and sensitivity -120 dBm, at 868 MHz. (a) Compute the maximum allowable path loss [2]. (b) Compute the free-space path loss at 5 km and the received power and margin [4]. (c) Compute the free-space path loss for a 2.4 GHz BLE link over 50 m, and the margin when P_tx = 4 dBm and sensitivity is -97 dBm (0 dBi antennas) [4]. (d) How much extra loss comes from doubling the distance in free space? [1] (e) Why is the free-space result optimistic? [3]\n\n**Q2. Log-distance model (10 marks)** At 868 MHz with the 1 m reference loss of the free-space model, compute the range for a path-loss budget of 140 dB with exponent n = 3, and for 130 dB with n = 2.5 [6]. For 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi with a 90 dB budget find the range for n = 2.5 and n = 3.5 [4].\n\n**Q3. LoRa (14 marks)** Use 125 kHz bandwidth, coding rate 4/5, 8-symbol preamble, explicit header and CRC. (a) Compute the symbol time and raw bit rate for SF10 [3]. (b) A 12-byte payload has 23 payload symbols at SF10 and 28 at SF8. Compute the time on air for both [5]. (c) With a 1 percent duty-cycle limit, how many messages per hour can the SF10 and SF8 nodes send? [3] (d) Compute the energy per message at 40 mA, 3.3 V for each [3].\n\n**Q4. MQTT (12 marks)** A farm sensor publishes to topic `farm/field3/soil` (16 bytes) with a 6-byte payload. (a) Give the size of the PUBLISH packet at QoS 0 and at QoS 1 (fixed header 2 bytes, topic length field 2 bytes, packet identifier 2 bytes at QoS 1) [3]. (b) Which subscriptions receive it: `farm/+/soil`, `farm/#`, `farm/field3`, `+/field3/+`? [4] (c) Describe QoS 0, 1 and 2 and the packets each uses [3]. (d) What is a last-will message and why is it useful? [2]\n\n**Q5. Data budget (10 marks)** 2,000 devices each send a 24-byte payload every 15 minutes using MQTT QoS 0 (topic from Q4). (a) Compute messages per day and per second [3]. (b) Compute the MQTT bytes per message, per day and per year (ignore TCP/TLS overhead) [4]. (c) Per device monthly traffic? [1] (d) Name two ways to reduce the traffic [2].\n\n**Q6. IEEE 802.15.4 and 6LoWPAN (10 marks)** (a) Compute the on-air time of a full 127-byte frame at 250 kbit/s [2]. (b) With a 25-byte MAC header, 40-byte IPv6 header and 8-byte UDP header, how many bytes remain for data? [2] (c) With 6LoWPAN header compression (2-byte IPHC and 4-byte compressed UDP) how many remain? [2] (d) Compute the maximum application throughput at 100 percent channel use with compression, ignoring acknowledgements and backoff [2] (e) Why does 6LoWPAN fragment packets? [2]\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Max PL = P_tx + G_tx + G_rx - sensitivity = 10 + 0 + 3 + 120 = **133 dB**.\n(b) Step 1: FSPL = 20 log₁₀(5) + 20 log₁₀(868) + 32.44 = 13.98 + 58.77 + 32.44 = **105.2 dB**.\nStep 2: P_rx = 10 + 0 + 3 - 105.2 = **-92.2 dBm**.\nStep 3: Margin = -92.2 - (-120) = **27.8 dB**, a comfortable fade margin (10 to 20 dB is typical).\n(c) FSPL = 20 log₁₀(0.05) + 20 log₁₀(2400) + 32.44 = -26.02 + 67.60 + 32.44 = **74.0 dB**. P_rx = 4 - 74.0 = -70.0 dBm; margin = -70.0 + 97 = **27.0 dB**.\n(d) **6 dB** (20 log₁₀ 2).\n(e) Real paths suffer multipath fading, shadowing by buildings and foliage, ground reflection, body or enclosure losses, antenna mismatch and polarization loss, and interference; the path-loss exponent exceeds 2 (typically 2.7 to 4 in cities and indoors), so the real range is much shorter than the free-space range.\n\n**Q2.**\nStep 1: PL(1 m, 868 MHz) = 20 log₁₀(0.001) + 58.77 + 32.44 = 31.2 dB. Then d = 10^((PL_budget - 31.2)/(10 n)) m. n = 3, 140 dB: (140 - 31.2)/30 = 3.627 → d = **4.23 km**. n = 2.5, 130 dB: (130 - 31.2)/25 = 3.952 → d = **8.9 km**.\nStep 2: For Wi-Fi: PL(1 m, 2.4 GHz) = -60 + 67.6 + 32.44 = 40.0 dB. n = 2.5: (90 - 40.0)/25 = 2.0 → **99.6 m**. n = 3.5: (90 - 40.0)/35 = 1.428 → **26.7 m**.\nStep 3: Walls and clutter shrink the range by a factor of nearly four.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) T_s = 2^10/125,000 = **8.192 ms**. Raw bit rate = SF × (4/5)/T_s = 10 × 0.8/8.192 ms = **976.6 bit/s**.\n(b) Step 1: T_air = T_s × (8 + 4.25 + N_payload).\nStep 2: SF10: 8.192 ms × (12.25 + 23) = **288.8 ms**.\nStep 3: SF8 (T_s = 2.048 ms): 2.048 × (12.25 + 28) = **82.4 ms**.\n(c) Step 1: 1 percent of 3600 s = 36 s.\nStep 2: SF10: 36/0.2888 = **124 messages/h**.\nStep 3: SF8: 36/0.0824 = **436 messages/h**.\n(d) E = V · I · t: SF10: 3.3 × 0.040 × 0.2888 = **38.1 mJ**; SF8: 3.3 × 0.040 × 0.0824 = **10.9 mJ**. The higher spreading factor costs 3.5 times the energy and airtime but buys about 5 dB of sensitivity (2.5 dB per step), so use the lowest SF that closes the link with margin (adaptive data rate).\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) QoS 0: 2 + 2 + 16 + 6 = **26 bytes**. QoS 1: 2 + 2 + 16 + 2 + 6 = **28 bytes** (plus a 4-byte PUBACK back).\n(b) `farm/+/soil` **yes** (+ matches field3). `farm/#` **yes** (# matches all remaining levels). `farm/field3` **no** (the topic has three levels). `+/field3/+` **yes**.\n(c) **QoS 0**: at most once, one PUBLISH, no acknowledgement. **QoS 1**: at least once, PUBLISH then PUBACK; the sender retransmits until acknowledged so duplicates are possible. **QoS 2**: exactly once, four-packet handshake PUBLISH, PUBREC, PUBREL, PUBCOMP.\n(d) The client registers a message at connection time that the broker publishes if the client disconnects unexpectedly (no clean DISCONNECT). It lets other clients learn of failures, for example publishing `offline` on a status topic.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Messages per day = 2000 × (1440/15) = 2000 × 96 = **192,000**; per second = 2000/900 s = **2.22 msg/s**.\n(b) Bytes per message = 2 + 2 + 16 + 24 = **44**. Per day 192,000 × 44 = **8.45 MB**; per year **3.08 GB**.\n(c) 96 × 30 × 44 = **126.7 kB/month** per device (MQTT only; TCP, TLS keep-alives and handshakes add more, so cellular plans must include that overhead).\n(d) Report less often or only on change (report-by-exception), batch several readings per message, use compact binary formats (CBOR, Protocol Buffers), compress topics, or aggregate at an edge gateway.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) 127 × 8/250,000 = **4.06 ms**.\n(b) 127 - 25 - 40 - 8 = **54 bytes**.\n(c) 127 - 25 - 2 - 4 = **96 bytes**.\n(d) Application throughput ≤ 250 kbit/s × 96/127 = **189 kbit/s** (the 54-byte uncompressed case would give 106 kbit/s); real throughput is far lower because of acknowledgements, backoff, interframe spacing and routing.\n(e) IPv6 requires a minimum MTU of 1280 bytes but 802.15.4 frames carry at most 127 bytes; 6LoWPAN fragments packets into several frames and reassembles them at the destination, at the cost of extra headers and the loss of the whole packet if one fragment is lost.\n\n# Assignment 3: Control, Estimation, Edge and Security (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. PID calculation (14 marks)** A discrete PID controller has K_p = 3, K_i = 2, K_d = 0.2 and T_s = 0.05 s, with u = K_p e + K_i I + K_d D, I ← I + e T_s, D = (e - e_prev)/T_s (D = 0 on the first step). The plant is y[k+1] = y[k] + 0.2 (u[k] - y[k]); y[0] = 0; the setpoint is 5. (a) Compute e, I, D and u for the first three steps [9]. (b) The actuator saturates at ±12. What does u become at step 0 and step 2, and what problem does saturation cause for the integral term? [3] (c) What does the large derivative value at step 1 tell you about derivative action? [2]\n\n**Q2. Discretization (8 marks)** A plant with time constant τ = 0.5 s is sampled with T_s = 0.1 s. (a) Give the exact (zero-order-hold) discrete pole and the forward-Euler approximation [3]. (b) Repeat for τ = 2 s, T_s = 0.2 s [2]. (c) What is the 2 percent settling time of the τ = 0.5 s system, and the time to reach 63 percent? [3]\n\n**Q3. Filtering (10 marks)** The sensor sequence is 0, 0, 10, 10, 10, 0. (a) Apply an exponential moving average with α = 0.3, starting from y[0] = x[0] [4]. (b) Apply a 4-point moving average to the last three possible windows [3]. (c) A temperature stream 20.1, 20.3, 85.0, 20.2, 20.4 contains a spike. Which filter removes it best and what is its output? [3]\n\n**Q4. Kalman filter (12 marks)** Estimate a constant with initial estimate x = 2, P = 2, process noise Q = 0.05 and measurement noise R = 1. Measurements: 3.0, 2.5, 3.5. For each step compute the gain, estimate and variance [9] and explain the trend in the gain [3].\n\n**Q5. Complementary filter (6 marks)** θ ← α(θ + ω T_s) + (1 - α) θ_acc with α = 0.95, T_s = 0.02 s, θ = 0 initially. Inputs (ω, θ_acc): (10, 0.5), (10, 0.45), (0, 0.6). Compute the three outputs.\n\n**Q6. Networked control and the edge (10 marks)** (a) A loop runs every 20 ms and a cloud round trip adds 100 ms. How many samples old is the data? Why is this a problem? [3] (b) A device streams video at 2 Mbit/s to the cloud; compute the monthly traffic, and compare with sending 20 detection events per hour of 50 bytes [4]. (c) Give three reasons to process at the edge [3].\n\n**Q7. Security and updates (10 marks)** (a) A fleet of 5,000 devices receives a 256 KB firmware image. Compute the total data for a full update and for a delta update that is 10 percent of the size [3]. (b) How long does one device take at an effective 100 kbit/s, and over BLE at 6.4 kbit/s? [3] (c) List four properties a secure OTA process needs [4].\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 0: e = 5 - 0 = 5; I = 5 × 0.05 = 0.25; D = 0; u = 3 × 5 + 2 × 0.25 + 0 = **15.5**; y[1] = 0 + 0.2 × 15.5 = 3.10. Step 1: e = 5 - 3.1 = 1.9; I = 0.25 + 1.9 × 0.05 = 0.345; D = (1.9 - 5)/0.05 = **-62**; u = 3 × 1.9 + 2 × 0.345 + 0.2 × (-62) = 5.7 + 0.69 - 12.4 = **-6.01**; y[2] = 3.1 + 0.2 × (-6.01 - 3.1) = 1.278. Step 2: e = 3.722; I = 0.345 + 3.722 × 0.05 = 0.531; D = (3.722 - 1.9)/0.05 = **36.44**; u = 11.166 + 1.062 + 7.288 = **19.52**.\n(b) Step 0: 15.5 → clipped to **12**. Step 2: 19.52 → clipped to **12**. When the actuator saturates but the integrator keeps accumulating the error, the integral term winds up and causes large overshoot after the saturation ends: use **anti-windup** (stop integrating or back-calculate when the output is saturated).\n(c) The derivative term reacts violently to changes in error (-62 when the error drops quickly), amplifying noise and flipping the output sign. In practice filter the derivative, apply it to the measurement instead of the error (no kick on setpoint changes), and use a small K_d or none.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Exact pole a = e^(-T_s/τ) = e^-0.2 = **0.8187**; forward Euler a = 1 - T_s/τ = **0.8** (b = 0.2 versus the exact 1 - 0.8187 = 0.1813).\n(b) τ = 2, T_s = 0.2: exact **0.9048**, Euler **0.9** (b = 0.0952): the approximation improves when T_s ≪ τ.\n(c) 2 percent settling ≈ 4τ = **2.0 s**; 63.2 percent at t = τ = **0.5 s**. Forward Euler becomes unstable if T_s > 2τ, so use T_s well below τ (rule of thumb τ/10 or smaller).\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) y = **0, 0, 3.0, 5.1, 6.57, 4.599** (for example y[2] = 0.3 × 10 + 0.7 × 0 = 3.0; y[3] = 0.3 × 10 + 0.7 × 3 = 5.1; y[4] = 3 + 0.7 × 5.1 = 6.57; y[5] = 0 + 0.7 × 6.57 = 4.599). The filter lags (time constant about 1/α = 3.3 samples) and never reaches 10.\n(b) The last three windows of the sequence (0, 0, 10, 10, 10, 0) are: (0,0,10,10) = **5.0**, (0,10,10,10) = **7.5**, (10,10,10,0) = **7.5**.\n(c) A **median filter** (window 5): sorted 20.1, 20.2, 20.3, 20.4, 85.0 → median **20.3**; the spike is rejected entirely, while a moving average would give 33.2 and smear the spike.\n\n**Q4.**\nStep 1: P = 2 + 0.05 = 2.05; K = 2.05/3.05 = **0.672**; x = 2 + 0.672 × (3.0 - 2) = **2.672**; P = (1 - 0.672) × 2.05 = **0.672**. Step 2: P = 0.722; K = 0.722/1.722 = **0.419**; x = 2.672 + 0.419 × (2.5 - 2.672) = **2.600**; P = **0.419**. Step 3: P = 0.469; K = 0.469/1.469 = **0.319**; x = 2.600 + 0.319 × (3.5 - 2.600) = **2.887**; P = **0.319**. The gain falls from 0.67 to 0.32 as the estimate becomes more certain, so each new measurement carries less weight; with Q > 0 it settles at a steady-state value rather than zero, which keeps the filter responsive to slow changes.\n\n**Q5.**\nStep 1: 0.95 × (0 + 10 × 0.02) + 0.05 × 0.5 = 0.19 + 0.025 = **0.215**. Step 2: 0.95 × (0.215 + 0.2) + 0.05 × 0.45 = 0.39425 + 0.0225 = **0.4168**. Step 3: 0.95 × (0.4168 + 0) + 0.05 × 0.6 = 0.3959 + 0.03 = **0.4259**.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) 100 ms / 20 ms = **5 samples** old (plus jitter). Delay in the loop reduces phase margin and can cause oscillation or instability; keep the fast loop local and use the cloud only for slow supervision.\n(b) 2 Mbit/s × 86,400 s = 21.6 GB per day, so about **648 GB per 30 days**. The events: 20 × 24 × 30 × 50 bytes = **0.72 MB per month**, about a million times less.\n(c) Latency (real-time response), bandwidth and cost, privacy (raw data stay local), resilience when the connection fails, energy (radio transmission is costly), and regulatory data-residency rules.\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) Full update: 5,000 × 262,144 bytes = **1.31 × 10⁹ bytes (1.31 GB)**. Delta at 10 percent: **0.131 GB**.\n(b) Step 1: 256 × 1024 × 8 = 2.10 Mbit.\nStep 2: At 100 kbit/s: **21 s**.\nStep 3: Over BLE at 6.4 kbit/s: 2.10 Mbit/6400 = 328 s ≈ **5.5 minutes**.\n(c) **Authenticity** (the image is signed and the bootloader verifies the signature with a public key held on the device); **integrity** (hash check on download, transport over TLS/DTLS); **rollback and downgrade protection** (monotonic version counter) plus an **A/B slot** or recovery partition so a failed update reverts safely; **resumability and power-fail safety**; staged rollout with monitoring; and protected signing keys (HSM).\n","exam":"# IoT and Cyber-Physical Systems: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\nUseful: FSPL(dB) = 20 log₁₀(d_km) + 20 log₁₀(f_MHz) + 32.44; 20 log₁₀ 868 = 58.77; thermistor T = 1/(1/298.15 + ln(R/R₀)/B) - 273.15 (kelvin to °C); 3.3 V reference; LoRa symbol time T_s = 2^SF/BW; e^-0.2 = 0.8187.\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** In the typical IoT layering, the component that aggregates data and may run local decisions near the sensors is the\nA) edge device or gateway  B) data warehouse  C) mobile app store  D) certificate authority\n\n**2.** The ideal SNR of an N-bit ADC due to quantization is approximately\nA) 20N dB  B) 3N dB  C) N dB  D) 6.02N + 1.76 dB\n\n**3.** Averaging 16 independent noisy ADC samples reduces random noise by a factor of\nA) 256  B) 2  C) 16  D) 4\n\n**4.** Sampling at 100 Hz, a 130 Hz tone appears at\nA) 230 Hz  B) 30 Hz  C) 70 Hz  D) 130 Hz\n\n**5.** A NTC thermistor's resistance\nA) falls with temperature, non-linearly  B) is independent of temperature  C) rises with temperature  D) falls linearly with temperature\n\n**6.** A device sleeping at 5 µA that wakes for a long, high-current radio burst is best improved by\nA) cutting the sleep current to 4 µA  B) using a larger case  C) reducing the burst duration or frequency  D) lowering the ADC resolution\n\n**7.** SPI differs from I²C in that SPI\nA) uses separate chip-select lines and is full duplex  B) is always slower  C) uses addresses in the data stream  D) has no clock\n\n**8.** Which statement about CAN bus is true?\nA) it has no error detection  B) it uses non-destructive arbitration by message ID  C) it is a wireless standard  D) it is a single-wire bus\n\n**9.** In the log-distance path-loss model, a larger exponent n means\nA) a longer range  B) less loss  C) loss grows faster with distance  D) a higher frequency\n\n**10.** LoRa's higher spreading factors give\nA) longer range, lower data rate and longer airtime  B) better video quality  C) higher data rate and shorter range  D) the same range\n\n**11.** Which radio technology is best suited to a battery sensor that sends a few bytes per hour over several kilometers?\nA) Bluetooth classic  B) Ethernet  C) Wi-Fi  D) LoRaWAN or NB-IoT\n\n**12.** In MQTT, a retained message is\nA) deleted after delivery  B) always encrypted  C) sent twice  D) stored by the broker and sent to new subscribers of that topic\n\n**13.** The MQTT last-will message is published by the broker when a client\nA) publishes at QoS 2  B) disconnects unexpectedly  C) subscribes  D) changes topic\n\n**14.** 6LoWPAN is needed because\nA) LoRa has no gateways  B) Wi-Fi is insecure  C) IPv4 has run out of addresses  D) IPv6 requires a larger frame than IEEE 802.15.4 supplies, so headers are compressed and packets fragmented\n\n**15.** For a discretized first-order plant, the forward-Euler approximation is accurate when\nA) the plant is unstable  B) T_s is much smaller than τ  C) T_s is much larger than τ  D) the gain is zero\n\n**16.** Derivative kick on a setpoint change can be avoided by\nA) applying the derivative term to the measurement instead of the error  B) removing the sensor  C) lowering the voltage  D) increasing K_i\n\n**17.** The Kalman gain tends to decrease over time for a constant state because\nA) the sensor degrades  B) the filter is unstable  C) Q increases  D) the estimate becomes more certain, so new measurements get less weight\n\n**18.** A digital twin is\nA) a live software model of a physical asset used for monitoring and simulation  B) a pair of identical firmware images  C) a backup battery  D) a duplicate sensor\n\n**19.** An A/B firmware slot scheme mainly provides\nA) safe rollback if an update fails  B) double the flash speed  C) lower power consumption  D) encryption of data\n\n**20.** A unique per-device credential is preferred to a shared fleet password because\nA) compromising one device does not expose the others  B) it never expires  C) it needs no storage  D) it is shorter\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. ADC (5 marks)** A 16-bit ADC has a 5 V reference. Compute the LSB size, the ideal quantization SNR and the code for 1.25 V.\n\n**B2. Aliasing (5 marks)** A 8 kHz sinusoid is sampled at 12 kHz. At what frequency does it appear? What is the minimum sampling rate that avoids aliasing, and what additional hardware is needed in practice?\n\n**B3. Battery life (5 marks)** A node with a 3000 mAh battery wakes every 20 minutes for 3 s at 30 mA and sleeps at 4 µA. Compute the average current and the ideal life in years.\n\n**B4. Link budget (5 marks)** A LoRa node transmits at 14 dBm with 2 dBi antennas at both ends, the receiver sensitivity is -125 dBm and the distance is 2 km at 868 MHz. Compute the free-space path loss, received power and margin.\n\n**B5. MQTT packet size (5 marks)** A PUBLISH has topic `grid/sub4/volt` and an 8-byte payload. Give its size at QoS 0 and QoS 1 (fixed header 2 bytes, topic length 2, packet id 2 at QoS 1).\n\n**B6. Discretization (5 marks)** A plant has τ = 0.25 s and the controller runs at T_s = 0.05 s. Give the exact and forward-Euler discrete poles and the 2 percent settling time.\n\n**B7. 6LoWPAN (5 marks)** An IEEE 802.15.4 frame has 127 bytes, a 25-byte MAC header, and with 6LoWPAN compression a 2-byte IP header and 4-byte UDP header. How many data bytes fit, and how long is the full frame on air at 250 kbit/s?\n\n**B8. Secure updates (5 marks)** List five requirements for a secure over-the-air firmware update process.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 40 marks)\n\n**C1. Thermistor measurement chain (10 marks)** A 10 kΩ NTC (B = 3950 K) is the lower resistor of a divider with a fixed 10 kΩ upper resistor to 3.3 V and is read by a 12-bit, 3.3 V ADC. (a) Find the temperature for ADC codes 1500 and 3000 [4]. (b) At 25 °C the sensitivity is dR/dT = -R B/T². Compute it, then dV/dT of the divider and the number of ADC codes per °C and the temperature per code [4]. (c) How does averaging 16 samples help, and what can it not fix? [2]\n\n**C2. Energy budget and harvesting (10 marks)** A node (3.3 V) wakes every 10 minutes for 2 s at 40 mA and sleeps at 5 µA, with a 2400 mAh battery. (a) Compute the average current, average power and battery life [4]. (b) Halving the active time to 1 s: new average current and life [2]. (c) Size a solar panel for the original node with 5 equivalent full-sun hours and 70 percent efficiency [2]. (d) How much storage gives three days of autonomy? [2]\n\n**C3. Choosing a radio (10 marks)** A node must send a 20-byte reading every 15 minutes from a field 3 km from the nearest gateway, powered by a battery for 5 years. A LoRa link has 125 kHz bandwidth; time on air for 20 bytes is 56.6 ms at SF7 and 1,319 ms at SF12; the radio draws 40 mA at 3.3 V when transmitting. (a) Compute the symbol time and raw bit rate for SF7 and SF12 [3]. (b) With the 1 percent duty-cycle limit, find the maximum messages per hour at each SF [2]. (c) Energy per message at each SF [2]. (d) Compare with Wi-Fi and BLE for this application and recommend a technology [3].\n\n**C4. Control loop with delay (10 marks)** A discrete PI controller (K_p = 1.5, K_i = 1, T_s = 0.1 s, I ← I + e T_s, u = K_p e + K_i I) controls the plant y[k+1] = y[k] + 0.25 (u[k] - y[k]) with y[0] = 0 and setpoint 6. (a) Compute e, I, u and y for the first three steps [6]. (b) The fast loop runs every 10 ms; if the controller is moved to the cloud with a 40 ms round trip, how many samples of delay result and why is that harmful? Suggest an architecture [4].\n\n**C5. Estimation and security (10 marks)** (a) A constant is estimated by a scalar Kalman filter with x = 0, P = 1, Q = 0.01, R = 0.5 and measurements 1.0, 1.2, 0.8. Compute gain, estimate and variance for each step [6]. (b) Describe four security controls for a smart-meter fleet and the threat each one addresses [4].\n\n## Answer key and mark scheme\n\n### Section A\n1. A 2. D 3. D 4. B 5. A 6. C 7. A 8. B 9. C 10. A 11. D 12. D 13. B 14. D 15. B 16. A 17. D 18. A 19. A 20. A\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.** LSB = 5/65,536 = **76.3 µV** [2]. SNR = 6.02 × 16 + 1.76 = **98.1 dB** [1]. Code = 1.25/5 × 65,536 = **16,384** [2].\n\n**B2.** The alias is |8000 - 12,000| = **4 kHz** [2]. The minimum rate is above 2 × 8 kHz = **16 kHz** [1]. In practice an analog anti-alias low-pass filter before the ADC (and sampling comfortably above the Nyquist rate) is required [2].\n\n**B3.** I_avg = (30 mA × 3 s + 0.004 mA × 1197 s)/1200 s = (90 + 4.79)/1200 = **0.0790 mA** [3]. Life = 3000/0.0790 = 37,980 h = **4.34 years** [2].\n\n**B4.** FSPL = 20 log₁₀(2) + 58.77 + 32.44 = 6.02 + 58.77 + 32.44 = **97.2 dB** [2]. P_rx = 14 + 2 + 2 - 97.2 = **-79.2 dBm** [2]. Margin = -79.2 + 125 = **45.8 dB** (comfortable, though real propagation is worse than free space) [1].\n\n**B5.** Topic length 14. QoS 0: 2 + 2 + 14 + 8 = **26 bytes** [2]. QoS 1: 2 + 2 + 14 + 2 + 8 = **28 bytes** (and a 4-byte PUBACK returns) [3].\n\n**B6.** Exact a = e^(-0.05/0.25) = e^-0.2 = **0.8187** [2]. Euler a = 1 - 0.2 = **0.8** [1]. Settling (2 percent) ≈ 4τ = **1.0 s** (20 samples) [2].\n\n**B7.** 127 - 25 - 2 - 4 = **96 bytes** [3]. Time = 127 × 8/250,000 = **4.06 ms** [2].\n\n**B8.** Any five of: signed firmware verified by the bootloader (authenticity); integrity check of the download; encrypted transport (TLS/DTLS); version counter with rollback/downgrade protection; A/B or recovery partition so failure reverts; power-fail-safe, resumable installation; staged rollout with monitoring and a kill switch; delta updates to save bandwidth; protection of signing keys (HSM) and an audit log [5].\n\n### Section C\n\n**C1.**\n(a) V = code × 3.3/4096; the NTC is the lower resistor, so R = 10k × V/(3.3 - V). Code 1500: V = 1.208 V, R = 5.78 kΩ, T = 1/(1/298.15 + ln(0.5778)/3950) - 273.15 = **37.9 °C**. Code 3000: V = 2.417 V, R = 27.37 kΩ, T = **3.9 °C** [4].\n(b) dR/dT = -10,000 × 3950/298.15² = **-444 Ω/°C**. For the divider V = 3.3 R/(10k + R): dV/dR = 3.3 × 10k/(20k)² = 8.25 × 10⁻⁵ V/Ω, so dV/dT = -444 × 8.25 × 10⁻⁵ = **-36.7 mV/°C**. One LSB = 0.806 mV, so there are 36.7/0.806 = **45.5 codes per °C**, i.e. **0.022 °C per code** (resolution is far better than the sensor's accuracy) [4].\n(c) Averaging 16 samples reduces random noise by 4 (about 2 bits of extra resolution if noise dithers the LSB). It cannot fix systematic errors: sensor tolerance, B-value error, self-heating, reference drift or non-linearity; those need calibration [2].\n\n**C2.**\n(a) I_avg = (40 mA × 2 s + 0.005 mA × 598 s)/600 s = (80 + 2.99)/600 = **0.138 mA**; P = 0.138 × 3.3 = **0.456 mW**; life = 2400/0.138 = 17,350 h = **1.98 years** [4].\n(b) Active 1 s: I_avg = (40 + 0.005 × 599)/600 = **0.0717 mA**; life = 2400/0.0717 = **3.82 years** (almost double) [2].\n(c) Daily energy = 0.456 mW × 24 h = 10.95 mWh; panel = 10.95/(5 × 0.7) = **3.13 mW** [2].\n(d) Three days: 0.138 mA × 72 h = **9.96 mAh** (at 3.3 V; about 33 mWh), with margin for efficiency and temperature, so a 15 to 20 mAh cell or a supercapacitor bank sized for the pulse current [2].\n\n**C3.**\n(a) SF7: T_s = 2⁷/125,000 = **1.024 ms**, raw rate = 7 × 0.8/1.024 ms = **5.47 kbit/s**. SF12: T_s = 4096/125,000 = **32.77 ms**, rate = 12 × 0.8/32.77 ms = **293 bit/s** [3].\n(b) 36 s/hour: SF7 → 36/0.0566 = **636 msg/h**; SF12 → 36/1.319 = **27 msg/h**. The requirement is 4 msg/h, so both satisfy the duty cycle [2].\n(c) SF7: 3.3 × 0.040 × 0.0566 = **7.5 mJ**; SF12: 3.3 × 0.040 × 1.319 = **174 mJ** (23 times more). A 3 km link may need SF9 to SF12 depending on the environment, so test with margin and use adaptive data rate [2].\n(d) Wi-Fi: range too short (tens of metres), high power, needs an access point. BLE: range too short for 3 km without a relay. **LoRaWAN** (or NB-IoT if cellular coverage and a subscription are acceptable) meets the range and the 5-year battery target: at 4 messages/hour the transmit energy is only about 30 mJ/hour at SF7 (2.5 µA average) and about 0.7 J/hour at SF12 (59 µA average, still giving about 4.7 years from 2400 mAh by itself), so choose the lowest spreading factor that closes the link with margin, and keep the sleep current in the microamp range [3].\n\n**C4.**\n(a) Step 0: e = 6; I = 0.6; u = 1.5 × 6 + 0.6 = **9.6**; y[1] = 0 + 0.25 × 9.6 = **2.4**. Step 1: e = 3.6; I = 0.6 + 0.36 = **0.96**; u = 5.4 + 0.96 = **6.36**; y[2] = 2.4 + 0.25 × (6.36 - 2.4) = **3.39**. Step 2: e = 2.61; I = 0.96 + 0.261 = **1.221**; u = 3.915 + 1.221 = **5.136**; y[3] = 3.39 + 0.25 × (5.136 - 3.39) = **3.8265** [6].\n(b) A 40 ms delay is **4 samples** (plus jitter), so the controller acts on stale data and the loop's phase margin shrinks, risking oscillation or instability and poor disturbance rejection. Keep the fast loop (10 ms) on the device or a local gateway and use the cloud only for slow supervisory tasks (setpoints, monitoring, optimization), with a safe local fallback if the connection drops [4].\n\n**C5.**\n(a) Step 1: P = 1.01; K = 1.01/1.51 = **0.669**; x = 0.669 × 1.0 = **0.669**; P = 0.331 × 1.01 = **0.334**. Step 2: P = 0.344; K = 0.344/0.844 = **0.408**; x = 0.669 + 0.408 × (1.2 - 0.669) = **0.886**; P = **0.204**. Step 3: P = 0.214; K = 0.214/0.714 = **0.300**; x = 0.886 + 0.300 × (0.8 - 0.886) = **0.860**; P = **0.150** [6].\n(b) Examples (about one mark each): per-device keys/certificates in a secure element (device impersonation, fleet-wide compromise); TLS/DTLS or application-layer encryption and authentication (eavesdropping, meter-data tampering, privacy of consumption profiles); secure boot and signed OTA updates (malicious firmware); tamper detection and sealed enclosures (physical tampering to under-report usage); rate limiting and anomaly detection at the head-end (denial of service, abnormal readings); network segmentation and least privilege for back-end systems [4].\n","notes":"# IoT and Cyber-Physical Systems: Complete Lecture Notes\n\nPrerequisites: microprocessors and embedded systems, signals and systems, control basics, computer networks. Every number below was computed in Python (path-loss and airtime formulas, battery arithmetic, filters, CRC) so you can reproduce it. Replace the example constants with the values from your real datasheets.\n\n## Chapter 1. What IoT and Cyber-Physical Systems Are\n\n### 1.1 Definitions\nA **cyber-physical system (CPS)** tightly couples computation and networking with physical processes: sensors measure the world, software decides, actuators change the world, and the loop closes through physics (a pacemaker, an anti-lock brake, a smart grid). The **Internet of Things (IoT)** is the connection of many such devices to the Internet to collect data and enable remote monitoring and control. IoT emphasizes connectivity and data at scale; CPS emphasizes real-time control and safety. Most real products are both.\n\n### 1.2 Layered architecture\n1. **Perception layer**: sensors, actuators, tags.\n2. **Device / edge layer**: microcontroller or gateway that reads sensors, filters, makes local decisions.\n3. **Network layer**: wired or wireless links and protocols.\n4. **Platform layer**: cloud or on-premise broker, device registry, storage, rules.\n5. **Application layer**: dashboards, analytics, machine learning, user apps, integration.\nCross-cutting: security, device management (provisioning, updates), and power.\n\n### 1.3 Design constraints that shape everything\nPower (battery or harvested), cost (a few dollars per node at volume), size, bandwidth, latency, reliability, security, lifetime (5 to 15 years), regulation (radio duty cycle, safety standards) and maintainability. A good designer starts from the **energy budget** and the **data budget**, then selects the radio, the processor and the protocol.\n\n## Chapter 2. Sensing, Actuation and Signal Conditioning\n\n### 2.1 Sensors and their specifications\nKnow accuracy, precision (repeatability), resolution, range, sensitivity, linearity, hysteresis, drift, response time and noise. Common devices: thermistor/RTD/thermocouple/IC temperature sensors, humidity (capacitive), pressure (piezoresistive), accelerometer/gyroscope (MEMS), light (photodiode), gas (metal oxide, electrochemical), distance (ultrasonic, time-of-flight), GPS, current (shunt, Hall effect). Actuators: relays, MOSFET drivers, DC/stepper/servo motors, valves, LEDs, buzzers.\n\n### 2.2 Analog-to-digital conversion\nAn N-bit ADC with reference V_ref has one least-significant bit (LSB) of V_ref/2ᴺ. A 12-bit ADC with 3.3 V gives **0.806 mV/LSB**; a 10-bit ADC with 5 V gives 4.88 mV/LSB; a 10-bit ADC with 1.1 V reference gives 1.074 mV/LSB; a 16-bit ADC with 3.3 V gives 0.050 mV/LSB. The ideal signal-to-quantization-noise ratio is 6.02N + 1.76 dB: 74 dB for 12 bits, 98 dB for 16. Quantization noise is LSB/√12 rms (0.23 mV for the 12-bit, 3.3 V case). Code 2048 corresponds to 1.650 V and code 1000 to 0.806 V; 1.0 V reads as 1241.\n\n**Oversampling and averaging.** Averaging N independent noisy samples reduces random noise by √N (16 samples reduce 1 mV rms noise to 0.25 mV), and oversampling by 4ᵏ with decimation adds about k bits of resolution: 16 samples take a 10-bit conversion to roughly 12 bits (when noise dithers the LSB).\n\n### 2.3 Sampling and aliasing\nSampling at f_s represents frequencies below f_s/2 (Nyquist). A component above f_s/2 folds to |f - k f_s|. A 60 Hz hum sampled at 50 Hz appears at 10 Hz; 130 Hz sampled at 100 Hz appears at 30 Hz; 7 kHz sampled at 10 kHz appears at 3 kHz. Use an analog **anti-aliasing low-pass filter** before the ADC with cutoff below f_s/2 and sample at 2.5 to 10 times the highest frequency of interest. Vibration monitoring of a 400 Hz signal needs f_s above 800 Hz and in practice 2 kHz or more.\n\n### 2.4 Worked example: NTC thermistor with a voltage divider\nA 10 kΩ NTC (B = 3950 K) follows R(T) = R₀ exp[B(1/T - 1/T₀)] with T in kelvin and T₀ = 298.15 K. Computed resistances: 0 °C → 33.6 kΩ; 25 °C → 10.0 kΩ; 50 °C → 3.59 kΩ; 100 °C → 0.70 kΩ. Place a fixed 10 kΩ resistor to 3.3 V and the NTC to ground so that V_out = 3.3 · R_NTC/(10 k + R_NTC), read by a 12-bit ADC. Code 2048 gives V = 1.650 V, R = 10 kΩ and T = 25.0 °C. Code 1500 gives 1.208 V, R = 5.78 kΩ, T = **37.9 °C**. Code 3000 gives 2.417 V, R = 27.4 kΩ, T = **3.9 °C**. Inverse: T = 1/(1/T₀ + ln(R/R₀)/B). The sensitivity at 25 °C is dR/dT = -R B/T² = -444 Ω/°C (about -4.4 percent per °C), which in this divider is -36.7 mV/°C or about 45 ADC codes per °C; the response is non-linear, so use the equation (or a lookup table) in software and calibrate against a reference.\n\n### 2.5 Conditioning and actuation basics\nAmplify small signals (instrumentation amplifier for bridges), filter noise, level-shift, and isolate noisy loads. Drive inductive loads (relays, motors) through a MOSFET or transistor with a flyback diode. Generate analog-like outputs with PWM. Always debounce mechanical switches (5 to 20 ms in software or an RC filter).\n\n## Chapter 3. Embedded Platforms and Power\n\n### 3.1 Processor choices\nMicrocontrollers (ARM Cortex-M, ESP32, nRF52, RISC-V) for most nodes; application processors (Cortex-A, Raspberry Pi class) with Linux for gateways and heavier edge computing. Select by ADC/peripherals, radio integration, memory (flash for firmware and an OTA slot, RAM for buffers and the network stack), sleep current and ecosystem. Real-time operating systems (FreeRTOS, Zephyr) provide tasks, queues and timers.\n\n### 3.2 Low-power design\nSpend most of the time asleep: average current I_avg = (I_active · t_active + I_sleep · t_sleep)/T. **Worked example.** A node wakes every 10 minutes for 2 s at 40 mA and sleeps at 5 µA: I_avg = (0.040 × 2 + 5×10⁻⁶ × 598)/600 = **0.138 mA**. A 2400 mAh battery lasts 2400/0.138 = 17,350 h = 723 days = **1.98 years**. Waking every 5 minutes doubles the active share: I_avg = 0.272 mA and life drops to **1.0 year**. Sleeping forever at 5 µA would last 55 years, so the **active burst dominates**; shorten it (fast connect, small payloads, lower TX power, batching) before optimizing the sleep current. One transmission burst costs 3.3 V × 40 mA × 2 s = 0.264 J.\n\nMore variants: 1000 mAh, 15 mA for 0.5 s every 60 s, sleep 10 µA → 0.135 mA, **0.85 years**; the same node reporting hourly → 0.012 mA, **9.4 years** (battery self-discharge and shelf life then limit it, often to about 10 years). A coin cell (220 mAh) at 20 mA for 0.1 s every 10 s with 2 µA sleep averages 0.20 mA and lasts only 0.12 years (about 45 days); coin cells suit very low duty cycles and low pulse currents.\n\n### 3.3 Energy harvesting\nSolar, vibration, thermal and RF sources give microwatts to milliwatts. A node averaging 0.456 mW (the 10-minute example) at 3.3 V needs about 3.1 mW of panel output under a 5-peak-sun-hours day with 70 percent system efficiency (0.456 × 24 / 5 / 0.7). Indoor light harvesting at about 50 µW covers only 11 percent of that load, so it supplements a battery or supports much lower duty cycles. Store energy in a supercapacitor or rechargeable cell with a power-management IC.\n\n## Chapter 4. Wired and Short-Range Buses\n\n- **UART**: asynchronous, 8N1 means 10 bit-times per byte (start, 8 data, stop). At 9600 baud: 960 bytes/s, so 100 bytes take 104 ms; at 115200 baud they take 8.7 ms.\n- **SPI**: synchronous, four wires (SCLK, MOSI, MISO, CS), full duplex, fast. A 16-bit transfer at 8 MHz takes 2 µs. Each extra device needs another chip-select.\n- **I²C**: two wires (SDA, SCL), open-drain with pull-ups, 7-bit addresses, multiple devices on one bus. A register read of 2 bytes = START, address+W (9 clocks including ACK), register (9), repeated START, address+R (9), two data bytes (18) = 45 clocks: **450 µs at 100 kHz**, 112.5 µs at 400 kHz. The rise time is about 0.8473 · R · C: 4.7 kΩ with 100 pF gives 398 ns (within the 1000 ns limit for standard mode); with 400 pF it is 1.59 µs, too slow, so lower the pull-up to below about 2.95 kΩ or shorten the bus. Do not exceed the sink current (typically 3 mA).\n- **CAN**: differential two-wire multi-master bus with arbitration by message ID, used in vehicles and industry; robust against noise. **RS-485/Modbus RTU** is common in factories. **1-Wire** for simple sensors. **USB** and **Ethernet** for gateways.\n- **CRC** error detection: CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE (polynomial 0x1021, initial 0xFFFF) of the ASCII string \"123456789\" is **0x29B1**, the standard check value. Protect every wire or radio frame with a CRC and verify it.\n\n## Chapter 5. Wireless Connectivity\n\n### 5.1 Path loss and link budget\nFree-space path loss: FSPL(dB) = 20 log₁₀(d_km) + 20 log₁₀(f_MHz) + 32.44. Examples: 1 km at 868 MHz → **91.2 dB**; 10 km at 868 MHz → 111.2 dB; 100 m at 2.4 GHz → 80.0 dB; 5 km at 433 MHz → 99.1 dB. Doubling distance adds 6 dB; doubling frequency adds 6 dB.\n\nLink budget: P_rx = P_tx + G_tx + G_rx - PL - losses. Link works if P_rx exceeds the receiver sensitivity by a **fade margin** (10 to 20 dB). With P_tx = 14 dBm, antenna gains 2 dBi at each end and sensitivity -137 dBm (LoRa SF12, 125 kHz) the maximum allowable path loss is 14 + 2 + 2 + 137 = **155 dB**. At 1 km in free space the received power would be 14 + 4 - 91.2 = -73.2 dBm.\n\nReal environments are lossier than free space; use the **log-distance model** PL(d) = PL(d₀) + 10 n log₁₀(d/d₀), with exponent n = 2 in free space, 2.7 to 3.5 in suburban or urban areas and 4 or more indoors with walls. Using d₀ = 1 m (PL = 31.2 dB at 868 MHz), allowable loss 145 dB (155 less a 10 dB margin): n = 2 → 489 km (unrealistic, line of sight only); n = 2.7 → **16 km**; n = 3.5 → **1.8 km**. For BLE at 2.4 GHz (1 m loss 40.0 dB), 0 dBm transmit power, -95 dBm sensitivity and 10 dB margin: n = 2 gives 177 m, n = 3 gives **32 m**. Moral: range claims depend on environment, antenna and margin.\n\n**Shannon capacity**: C = B log₂(1 + SNR). A 20 MHz Wi-Fi channel at 30 dB SNR: 199 Mbit/s upper bound. A 125 kHz LoRa channel at SNR = -20 dB (0.01): 1.79 kbit/s: low-rate links can work below the noise floor with spreading gain.\n\n### 5.2 Technology comparison\n- **Wi-Fi** (2.4/5 GHz): high rate, high power, existing infrastructure; use for mains-powered devices and camera-like data.\n- **Bluetooth Low Energy**: short range (10 to 50 m typical), low power, phone-friendly, mesh option. Advertising once per second at 10 mA for 1 ms averages about 11 µA. A 100 ms connection interval carrying four 20-byte packets gives 6.4 kbit/s.\n- **IEEE 802.15.4** (Zigbee, Thread, 6LoWPAN): 250 kbit/s at 2.4 GHz, 127-byte frames (4.06 ms on air), mesh networking, low power. IPv6 over it needs header compression (6LoWPAN): without compression only 127 - 25 (MAC) - 40 (IPv6) - 8 (UDP) = 54 bytes remain for data.\n- **LoRa / LoRaWAN** (sub-GHz, 868/915 MHz): long range (kilometers), very low data rate, years of battery life, star topology to gateways. **NB-IoT / LTE-M** (cellular): licensed spectrum, good penetration, subscription cost, power-saving modes (PSM, eDRX).\n- **Sub-GHz proprietary, Sigfox, Z-Wave, NFC/RFID** for niches. Choose by range, data rate, power, infrastructure and cost.\n\n### 5.3 LoRa in numbers\nThe symbol time is T_s = 2^SF / BW. For BW = 125 kHz: SF7 → 1.024 ms and raw bit rate SF·(4/5)/T_s = **5.47 kbit/s**; SF9 → 4.096 ms, 1.76 kbit/s; SF12 → 32.77 ms, **293 bit/s**. A higher spreading factor gains sensitivity (about 2.5 dB per step) at the cost of airtime. The time on air of a 20-byte payload (8-symbol preamble, explicit header, CRC on, coding rate 4/5, low-data-rate optimization for SF11/12) is **56.6 ms** at SF7, **185 ms** at SF9 and **1.32 s** at SF12. With the EU868 1 percent duty-cycle rule a device may transmit 36 s per hour: **636 messages per hour at SF7 but only 27 at SF12**. Energy per message at 40 mA and 3.3 V: 7.5 mJ (SF7) versus 174 mJ (SF12): adaptive data rate (ADR) puts devices near gateways on SF7 to save battery and capacity.\n\n### 5.4 Reliability of wireless links\nInterference, multipath fading, collisions (ALOHA-like channel access in LoRaWAN limits network capacity), and duty-cycle rules. Use acknowledgements and retransmission with backoff, forward error correction, channel hopping, and frame sequence numbers.\n\n## Chapter 6. Application Protocols and Data\n\n### 6.1 MQTT\nPublish/subscribe over TCP with a central **broker**. Topics are hierarchical (`home/livingroom/temp`); `+` matches one level and `#` the rest. Quality of service: **QoS 0** at most once (1 packet), **QoS 1** at least once (PUBLISH + PUBACK, may duplicate), **QoS 2** exactly once (four-packet handshake). Features: retained messages, last-will-and-testament (the broker announces a client's unexpected disconnect), persistent sessions and keep-alive pings. Overhead is tiny: a PUBLISH with topic `home/temp` (9 bytes) and payload `23.5` (4 bytes) at QoS 0 is 2 (fixed header) + 2 (topic length) + 9 + 4 = **17 bytes** (19 at QoS 1 with the packet identifier, plus a 4-byte PUBACK), whereas a minimal HTTP/1.1 POST carrying the same four bytes is about **52 bytes** before TLS.\n\n### 6.2 CoAP, HTTP and others\n**CoAP** is a RESTful protocol over UDP with a 4-byte header, confirmable messages, observe (push notifications) and block-wise transfer: suited to constrained devices. HTTP/REST is universal but heavier. **AMQP** for enterprise queues, **LwM2M** for device management, **OPC UA** for industrial interoperability, **Matter** for smart homes. Payload formats: JSON (readable), CBOR and Protocol Buffers (compact).\n\n### 6.3 Data volume and cost\n1,000 devices sending 50 bytes per minute generate 72 MB per day (26 GB per year), only 16.7 messages per second: modest. A single 1080p camera at 2 Mbit/s produces 21.6 GB per day, which is why video is analysed at the edge and only events are sent. Estimate message rates, payload sizes, retention and cloud pricing early.\n\n### 6.4 Time-series data and analytics\nStore sensor data in a time-series database, downsample old data, apply alert rules and anomaly detection, and use machine learning for predictive maintenance. Timestamp at the device (synchronized by NTP or GPS) and include sequence numbers to detect loss.\n\n## Chapter 7. Edge, Fog and Cloud\n\nProcess locally when latency, bandwidth, privacy or offline operation matter; use the cloud for fleet-wide analytics, storage and model training. **Edge** (on the device or a gateway): filtering, event detection, TinyML inference, control. **Cloud**: aggregation and long-term storage. Example: transmitting only 20 events per hour of 50 bytes (about 24 kB per day) instead of 21.6 GB per day of continuous video. **TinyML**: quantize small neural networks (int8) to run in tens of kilobytes on a microcontroller. A **digital twin** is a live software model of a physical asset used for monitoring, simulation and what-if analysis.\n\n## Chapter 8. Control in Cyber-Physical Systems\n\n### 8.1 Feedback control and discretization\nA controller compares the setpoint with the measurement and acts on the error. Software implements it in discrete time with sample period T_s on a timer interrupt. A first-order plant dx/dt = -x + u, sampled with T_s = 0.1 s (zero-order hold) becomes x[k+1] = e^-0.1 x[k] + (1 - e^-0.1) u[k] = 0.9048 x[k] + 0.0952 u[k]; the simple Euler approximation uses 0.9 and 0.1. A first-order system with time constant τ reaches 63.2 percent of the final value after τ, 95 percent after 3τ, and settles to within 2 percent after about 4τ.\n\n### 8.2 The PID controller\nu = K_p e + K_i ∫e dt + K_d de/dt. In discrete form: I += e·T_s, D = (e - e_prev)/T_s. **Worked example.** K_p = 2, K_i = 1, K_d = 0.5, T_s = 0.1 s, setpoint 10, a plant y ← y + 0.1(u - y), initial y = 0. Step 0: e = 10, I = 1.0, D = 0 (no previous), u = 2×10 + 1.0 = **21.0**; y becomes 2.1. Step 1: e = 7.9, I = 1.79, D = (7.9 - 10)/0.1 = -21, u = 15.8 + 1.79 - 10.5 = **7.09**. The derivative term reacts to the change in error and damps the response but amplifies noise, so filter it. Practical details: **anti-windup** (clamp the integral when the actuator saturates), derivative on measurement to avoid setpoint kick, output limits, bumpless transfer, and fixed, jitter-free sampling. Tune with Ziegler-Nichols or by hand: raise K_p until it oscillates, add K_d to damp, K_i to remove steady-state error.\n\n### 8.3 Networked control, timing and real time\nNetwork delay and jitter degrade control. If the loop runs every 10 ms and a cloud round trip adds 30 ms, the controller acts on measurements that are three samples old, which can destabilize a fast loop. Keep time-critical loops **local**; use the network for supervision (setpoints, monitoring). Use real-time scheduling with priorities and bounded worst-case execution time, hardware timers and watchdogs. For safety-critical systems use deterministic networks (CAN, TSN, EtherCAT) and formal timing analysis.\n\n## Chapter 9. Estimation and Filtering\n\n### 9.1 Simple filters\n- **Moving average** of length 3: for the series 10, 10, 10, 20, 20, 20 the outputs are 10, 13.33, 16.67, 20 (it lags a step by about half the window).\n- **Exponential moving average** y[k] = α x[k] + (1 - α) y[k-1]: with α = 0.2 and the same series, y = 10, 10, 10, 12.0, 13.6, 14.88. Small α smooths strongly and lags more. Cheap: one multiply-add and one stored value.\n- **Median filter** removes spikes (outliers) without blurring edges.\n\n### 9.2 Sensor fusion: the complementary filter\nA gyro gives a smooth angular rate that drifts when integrated; an accelerometer gives a noisy but unbiased angle. Combine: θ = α(θ + ω T_s) + (1 - α) θ_acc with α = 0.98. Starting from θ = 10°, T_s = 0.01 s: with gyro 5°/s and accelerometer angle 10.5° → 10.059°; then (5°/s, 10.6°) → 10.119°; then (-2°/s, 10.4°) → 10.105°. High frequencies come from the gyro, low frequencies from the accelerometer.\n\n### 9.3 The Kalman filter (scalar)\nFor a constant state with process variance Q and measurement variance R: predict P ← P + Q; gain K = P/(P + R); update x ← x + K(z - x); P ← (1 - K)P. **Worked example.** x₀ = 0, P₀ = 1, Q = 0.01, R = 0.5, measurements 1.0, 1.2, 0.8. Step 1: P = 1.01, K = 0.669, x = **0.669**, P = 0.334. Step 2: P = 0.344, K = 0.408, x = **0.886**, P = 0.204. Step 3: K = 0.300, x = **0.860**, P = 0.150. The gain falls as confidence rises, so the filter trusts the model more and the new measurements less. The multidimensional Kalman filter (and the extended version for non-linear systems) fuses GPS, IMU and odometry in vehicles and robots.\n\n## Chapter 10. Security, Updates and Privacy for IoT\n\n- **Threats**: default passwords (the Mirai botnet), unencrypted traffic, unauthenticated firmware, exposed debug ports, insecure cloud APIs, physical tampering, supply-chain compromise. A compromised device can become a botnet node or a foothold into a network, or cause physical harm.\n- **Device identity**: unique per-device keys or certificates provisioned in manufacturing and stored in secure elements, TPMs or the microcontroller's trusted zone; no shared secrets across a fleet.\n- **Communication**: TLS or DTLS (MQTT over TLS on port 8883, CoAP over DTLS), authenticated and encrypted at the application layer; for LoRaWAN, the network and application session keys (AES-128).\n- **Secure boot and OTA updates**: the bootloader verifies the firmware signature (for example ECDSA or Ed25519) before running it; updates are signed, versioned, with rollback protection and an A/B image slot so a failed update can revert. Update time example: a 512 KB image takes 4.2 s over a 1 Mbit/s Wi-Fi link, 655 s (11 minutes) over a 6.4 kbit/s BLE connection, and 767 s (13 minutes) over LoRa SF7 at its raw rate, and in practice much longer under the duty-cycle limit, so use delta updates and avoid LoRa for large images.\n- **Principles**: least privilege, disable unused services, minimal attack surface, secure defaults, logging and anomaly detection, and a vulnerability disclosure process. Privacy: collect the minimum, anonymize, encrypt, and give users control (regulations such as GDPR apply).\n\n## Chapter 11. Reliability, Safety and Standards\n\n- **Reliability**: watchdog timers, brown-out detection, error-correcting memory, redundancy, graceful degradation, self-test. Availability = MTBF/(MTBF + MTTR).\n- **Functional safety**: hazard analysis (FMEA, fault trees), safety integrity levels (IEC 61508; ISO 26262 for cars; IEC 62304 for medical software), fail-safe states, independent safety monitors. Separate safety functions from convenience functions.\n- **Testing**: hardware-in-the-loop simulation, fault injection, field trials, long-duration soak tests, EMC compliance, radio certification.\n- **Standards**: IEEE 802.15.4, Bluetooth SIG, LoRa Alliance, MQTT (OASIS), IETF CoAP, Matter and Thread for homes, OPC UA and Modbus for industry.\n\n## Chapter 12. Design Method and Checklist\n\n1. Write requirements: what is sensed, how often, accuracy, latency, lifetime, environment, cost.\n2. Do the budgets: energy (I_avg and battery life), data (messages per day), link (path loss and margin), cost.\n3. Choose the sensor and ADC resolution (LSB smaller than the required resolution, after averaging).\n4. Choose the radio, protocol and topology; pick the cloud platform and data model.\n5. Design security (identity, encryption, secure boot, OTA) from the start.\n6. Prototype, measure real current with a power analyser, field-test range, iterate.\n7. Plan manufacturing test, provisioning, fleet management and end-of-life.\n\n**Common mistakes.** Ignoring the active burst when estimating battery life; trusting free-space range numbers; sampling without an anti-alias filter; blocking network calls in the control loop; no CRC or sequence numbers; shared default passwords; no OTA path; I²C pull-ups too large for the bus capacitance; floating inputs and missing decoupling capacitors; forgetting duty-cycle regulations; storing secrets in firmware images.\n","plan":"# IoT and Cyber-Physical Systems: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 12 hours per week (3 reading, 6 to 8 building and measuring, 1 review with the app's quizzes). This is a hardware-plus-software course: buy a microcontroller board with a radio (ESP32 or nRF52), a few sensors, a breadboard and a USB power meter, and build every week.\n\n## Textbooks\n- Alasdair Gilchrist, *Industry 4.0* and Perry Lea, *IoT and Edge Computing for Architects* (system-level views).\n- Edward Lee and Sanjit Seshia, *Introduction to Embedded Systems: A Cyber-Physical Systems Approach* (free PDF).\n- Rajeev Alur, *Principles of Cyber-Physical Systems*.\n- Karl Åström and Richard Murray, *Feedback Systems* (free PDF) and Åström and Hägglund on PID control.\n- Rob Faludi, *Building Wireless Sensor Networks*; Adam Dunkels' Contiki and Zephyr documentation.\n- Roger Light's Mosquitto/MQTT documentation; the MQTT 3.1.1/5.0 and CoAP (RFC 7252) specifications.\n\n## Free courses\n- Coursera: Internet of Things Specialization (UC San Diego) and Embedded Systems (UT Austin, edX).\n- Berkeley EECS 149 (Cyber-Physical Systems) open materials; Brian Douglas's Control Systems Lectures on YouTube.\n- Zephyr and ESP-IDF getting-started guides; Nordic Developer Academy (BLE and LTE-M/NB-IoT).\n- The Things Network learning pages (LoRaWAN); TinyML materials from Harvard and Pete Warden.\n- Andreas Spiess's YouTube channel for practical sensor, power and radio measurements.\n\n## Tools\nA development board (ESP32, nRF52 or STM32 with radio), sensors (temperature, humidity, IMU, light), a multimeter, a logic analyser or oscilloscope, a USB or shunt-based current meter, an MQTT broker (Mosquitto), Node-RED or Grafana for dashboards, and Python for analysis. A LoRa module and a gateway (or a public network) for the wireless weeks.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: IoT architecture and requirements.** Layers, use cases, constraints, budgets. Lab: write a requirements sheet for a node and estimate energy, data and cost.\n\n**Week 2: Sensors, ADC and signal conditioning.** Specifications, resolution, noise, thermistor equation, voltage dividers, calibration. Lab: build the thermistor circuit, convert ADC codes to temperature, and compare with a reference.\n\n**Week 3: Sampling theory in practice.** Nyquist, aliasing, anti-alias filters, oversampling, simple digital filters (EMA, moving average, median). Lab: sample a known tone below and above Nyquist and observe aliasing.\n\n**Week 4: Embedded platforms and low-power design.** Sleep modes, wake-up sources, interrupts, RTOS tasks, battery arithmetic. Lab: measure sleep and active currents and compute battery life; cut the active time.\n\n**Week 5: Buses and peripherals.** UART, SPI, I²C, CAN, Modbus, timing and pull-up sizing, CRC. Lab: read an I²C sensor, capture the bus on a logic analyser, check the clock count and timing.\n\n**Week 6: Wireless fundamentals.** Path loss, link budget, fading, Shannon, modulation basics. Lab: measure RSSI against distance and fit a path-loss exponent.\n\n**Week 7: Short-range wireless.** BLE, 802.15.4, Zigbee/Thread, Wi-Fi power modes. Lab: BLE advertising and a GATT service; measure average current.\n\n**Week 8: Long-range wireless.** LoRa/LoRaWAN, NB-IoT/LTE-M, duty cycle, adaptive data rate. Lab: compute airtimes; send LoRa packets at different spreading factors.\n\n**Week 9: Application protocols and data.** MQTT (topics, QoS, retained, last will), CoAP, HTTP, JSON versus CBOR, time-series storage. Lab: build a publish/subscribe pipeline with Mosquitto and a Grafana dashboard.\n\n**Week 10: Edge and cloud.** Gateways, edge processing, TinyML, digital twins, cost and bandwidth estimates. Lab: run a small quantized model on the microcontroller and send only events.\n\n**Week 11: Control in CPS.** Discretization, PID design and tuning, anti-windup, timing and jitter. Lab: control a heater or motor with PID; log step responses and tune.\n\n**Week 12: Estimation and sensor fusion.** Complementary filter, Kalman filter, IMU attitude estimation. Lab: fuse accelerometer and gyro; compare with a Kalman implementation.\n\n**Week 13: Security, updates, reliability and safety.** Secure boot, device identity, OTA, watchdogs, FMEA, functional-safety standards. Lab: implement a signed update check and an A/B rollback test.\n\n**Week 14: Integration and project.** Final project: build a complete node (sensing, low power, wireless, cloud, security, a control or alerting function), present budgets versus measurements and a failure analysis.\n\n## Assessment pattern used in this course\nThree assignments (sensing, conversion, buses and power; wireless links, protocols and data; control, estimation, edge and security), a class test, and a three-hour final. Practise by redoing each calculation from the notes with your own datasheet values and then measuring the real device.\n\n## Study tips\n- Start every design with the energy budget, link budget and data budget; the numbers choose the architecture.\n- Measure, do not guess: a USB power meter and a logic analyser will teach more than datasheets alone.\n- Keep time-critical loops local and deterministic; keep cloud interactions asynchronous.\n- Build security and updates in from the first prototype: retrofitting them is much harder.\n- Log everything during field tests (battery voltage, RSSI, retries, resets) so failures can be explained.\n","test":"# IoT and Cyber-Physical Systems: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed, no notes. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each). Useful: FSPL(dB) = 20 log₁₀(d_km) + 20 log₁₀(f_MHz) + 32.44; log₁₀ 2.4 = 0.380 (so 20 log₁₀ 2400 = 67.6); 3.3 V reference, 12-bit ADC unless stated.\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** A cyber-physical system is best described as\nA) a closed-circuit television network  B) a cloud database  C) a social network  D) computation and networking tightly coupled with a physical process\n\n**2.** The LSB of a 12-bit ADC with a 3.3 V reference is about\nA) 8 mV  B) 0.08 mV  C) 0.8 mV  D) 3.3 mV\n\n**3.** To avoid aliasing you should\nA) sample as slowly as possible  B) raise the reference voltage  C) use a bigger battery  D) use an analog low-pass filter before the ADC\n\n**4.** A 60 Hz tone sampled at 50 Hz appears at\nA) 25 Hz  B) 60 Hz  C) 10 Hz  D) 110 Hz\n\n**5.** In a battery-powered sensor that wakes briefly to transmit, the biggest lever on battery life is usually\nA) the colour of the case  B) the sleep current only  C) shortening the active burst and reducing how often it occurs  D) the baud rate of the debug port\n\n**6.** The I²C bus uses\nA) one wire only  B) a differential pair with CAN arbitration  C) four wires and no pull-ups  D) two open-drain wires with pull-up resistors\n\n**7.** An 8N1 UART sends each byte as\nA) 10 bits  B) 8 bits  C) 9 bits  D) 12 bits\n\n**8.** Doubling the distance in free space adds how much path loss?\nA) 6 dB  B) 12 dB  C) 3 dB  D) 20 dB\n\n**9.** LoRa trades data rate for range mainly by\nA) changing the voltage  B) increasing the spreading factor  C) using Wi-Fi  D) adding more antennas\n\n**10.** In MQTT the entity that routes messages between publishers and subscribers is the\nA) DNS  B) router  C) gateway  D) broker\n\n**11.** MQTT QoS 1 means\nA) at least once  B) exactly once  C) never delivered  D) at most once\n\n**12.** The topic filter `home/+/temp` matches\nA) home/kitchen/oven/temp  B) house/kitchen/temp  C) home/kitchen/temp  D) home/temp\n\n**13.** CoAP is typically carried over\nA) SMTP  B) FTP  C) TCP  D) UDP\n\n**14.** In a PID controller the integral term mainly\nA) limits the actuator  B) removes steady-state error  C) filters noise  D) predicts future error\n\n**15.** Integral windup is prevented by\nA) a longer cable  B) anti-windup (clamping the integrator when the output saturates)  C) a bigger K_d  D) lower baud rate\n\n**16.** Running a fast control loop through a cloud round trip is usually a bad idea because\nA) JSON is slow  B) the cloud is too expensive  C) delay and jitter reduce stability margin  D) cloud servers cannot count\n\n**17.** The complementary filter combines\nA) two batteries  B) a gyroscope (good at high frequency) with an accelerometer (good at low frequency)  C) MQTT and CoAP  D) two thermistors\n\n**18.** A median filter is especially good at removing\nA) isolated spikes  B) slow drift  C) a constant offset  D) quantization\n\n**19.** Secure boot ensures that\nA) the Wi-Fi password is long  B) only firmware with a valid signature is run  C) the device boots faster  D) the battery lasts longer\n\n**20.** The Mirai botnet mainly exploited IoT devices with\nA) quantum computers  B) low batteries  C) default or weak credentials  D) faulty ADCs\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Sensing and power (10 marks)** (a) A voltage of 2.5 V is read by a 12-bit, 3.3 V ADC. What code results? [2] (b) A node has a 1500 mAh battery, wakes every 10 minutes for 1 s at 20 mA and sleeps at 10 µA. Find the average current and the ideal life in years [5]. (c) State two reasons the real life will be shorter [3].\n\n**B2. Link budget (10 marks)** A BLE sensor transmits at 0 dBm (0 dBi antennas) to a hub with sensitivity -90 dBm at 2.4 GHz. (a) Compute the free-space path loss at 20 m [3]. (b) Compute the received power and fade margin [3]. (c) The path loss at 40 m is 72.1 dB. How much margin is left and how does that compare with typical required margins? [2] (d) Why can walls make the real range much smaller? [2]\n\n**B3. Data budget and MQTT (10 marks)** 500 devices each publish a 60-byte message every 10 minutes. (a) Find messages per day and per second [3]. (b) Find the data per day in MB [2]. (c) Give the size of a QoS 0 PUBLISH carrying topic `plant/line2/temp` (16 bytes) with a 5-byte payload, and at QoS 1 (fixed header 2, topic length 2, packet id 2 at QoS 1) [3]. (d) Which of QoS 0, 1 or 2 would you choose for a periodic temperature reading and why? [2]\n\n**B4. Filtering and control (10 marks)** (a) Apply an exponential moving average with α = 0.5 to 0, 8, 8, 8 starting from y = 0 [3]. (b) A PID with K_p = 2, K_i = 0.5, K_d = 0 and T_s = 0.1 s sees a constant error e = 4 on the first step (I starts at 0, I ← I + e T_s). Compute the output [3]. (c) The actuator is limited to ±6. What is the applied output and what should happen to the integral? [2] (d) Why is derivative action often filtered? [2]\n\n## Answer key and solutions\n\n### Section A\n1. D 2. C 3. D 4. C 5. C 6. D 7. A 8. A 9. B 10. D 11. A 12. C 13. D 14. B 15. B 16. C 17. B 18. A 19. B 20. C\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) Code = 2.5/3.3 × 4096 = **3103** [2].\n(b) I_avg = (20 mA × 1 s + 0.010 mA × 599 s)/600 s = (20 + 5.99)/600 = **0.0433 mA** [3]. Life = 1500/0.0433 = 34,630 h = **3.95 years** [2].\n(c) Any two: battery self-discharge and capacity loss at low temperature; radio retries and connection set-up taking longer than assumed; the regulator and sensor quiescent currents; pulse-current derating; ageing [3].\n\n**B2.**\n(a) FSPL = 20 log₁₀(0.02) + 20 log₁₀(2400) + 32.44 = -33.98 + 67.60 + 32.44 = **66.06 dB** [3].\n(b) P_rx = 0 - 66.06 = **-66.1 dBm**; margin = -66.1 - (-90) = **23.9 dB** [3].\n(c) Margin = 0 - 72.1 + 90 = **17.9 dB**: acceptable against the usual 10 to 20 dB fade margin but with little room for walls or interference [2].\n(d) Real paths have a path-loss exponent above 2 and add wall, body and furniture attenuation plus multipath fading, so the loss grows much faster with distance than in free space [2].\n\n**B3.**\n(a) 500 × 144 = **72,000 messages per day**; 500/600 = **0.83 per second** [3].\n(b) 72,000 × 60 B = **4.32 MB/day** [2].\n(c) QoS 0: 2 + 2 + 16 + 5 = **25 bytes**; QoS 1: 2 + 2 + 16 + 2 + 5 = **27 bytes** [3].\n(d) **QoS 0** (or QoS 1 if every reading matters): a periodic reading is replaced by the next one soon, so losing one is tolerable and QoS 0 saves energy and airtime; QoS 2's four-packet handshake is wasteful [2].\n\n**B4.**\n(a) y[0] = 0; y[1] = 0.5 × 8 + 0.5 × 0 = **4**; y[2] = 0.5 × 8 + 0.5 × 4 = **6**; y[3] = 0.5 × 8 + 0.5 × 6 = **7** [3].\n(b) I = 4 × 0.1 = 0.4; u = 2 × 4 + 0.5 × 0.4 = 8 + 0.2 = **8.2** [3].\n(c) The applied output is clipped to **6**; the integrator should stop accumulating (anti-windup) while the actuator is saturated, otherwise it winds up and causes overshoot [2].\n(d) Differentiation amplifies high-frequency noise and reacts violently to steps in the error; a low-pass filter on the derivative (and applying it to the measurement) keeps the output usable [2].\n"},mcq:[{"q":"A cyber-physical system is best described as","options":["a closed-circuit television network","a cloud database","a social network","computation and networking tightly coupled with a physical process"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The LSB of a 12-bit ADC with a 3.3 V reference is about","options":["8 mV","0.08 mV","0.8 mV","3.3 mV"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"To avoid aliasing you should","options":["sample as slowly as possible","raise the reference voltage","use a bigger battery","use an analog low-pass filter before the ADC"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A 60 Hz tone sampled at 50 Hz appears at","options":["25 Hz","60 Hz","10 Hz","110 Hz"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a battery-powered sensor that wakes briefly to transmit, the biggest lever on battery life is usually","options":["the colour of the case","the sleep current only","shortening the active burst and reducing how often it occurs","the baud rate of the debug port"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The I²C bus uses","options":["one wire only","a differential pair with CAN arbitration","four wires and no pull-ups","two open-drain wires with pull-up resistors"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"An 8N1 UART sends each byte as","options":["10 bits","8 bits","9 bits","12 bits"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Doubling the distance in free space adds how much path loss?","options":["6 dB","12 dB","3 dB","20 dB"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"LoRa trades data rate for range mainly by","options":["changing the voltage","increasing the spreading factor","using Wi-Fi","adding more antennas"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In MQTT the entity that routes messages between publishers and subscribers is the","options":["DNS","router","gateway","broker"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"MQTT QoS 1 means","options":["at least once","exactly once","never delivered","at most once"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The topic filter `home/+/temp` matches","options":["home/kitchen/oven/temp","house/kitchen/temp","home/kitchen/temp","home/temp"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"CoAP is typically carried over","options":["SMTP","FTP","TCP","UDP"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a PID controller the integral term mainly","options":["limits the actuator","removes steady-state error","filters noise","predicts future error"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Integral windup is prevented by","options":["a longer cable","anti-windup (clamping the integrator when the output saturates)","a bigger K_d","lower baud rate"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Running a fast control loop through a cloud round trip is usually a bad idea because","options":["JSON is slow","the cloud is too expensive","delay and jitter reduce stability margin","cloud servers cannot count"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The complementary filter combines","options":["two batteries","a gyroscope (good at high frequency) with an accelerometer (good at low frequency)","MQTT and CoAP","two thermistors"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A median filter is especially good at removing","options":["isolated spikes","slow drift","a constant offset","quantization"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Secure boot ensures that","options":["the Wi-Fi password is long","only firmware with a valid signature is run","the device boots faster","the battery lasts longer"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The Mirai botnet mainly exploited IoT devices with","options":["quantum computers","low batteries","default or weak credentials","faulty ADCs"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In the typical IoT layering, the component that aggregates data and may run local decisions near the sensors is the","options":["edge device or gateway","data warehouse","mobile app store","certificate authority"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The ideal SNR of an N-bit ADC due to quantization is approximately","options":["20N dB","3N dB","N dB","6.02N + 1.76 dB"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Averaging 16 independent noisy ADC samples reduces random noise by a factor of","options":["256","2","16","4"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Sampling at 100 Hz, a 130 Hz tone appears at","options":["230 Hz","30 Hz","70 Hz","130 Hz"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A NTC thermistor's resistance","options":["falls with temperature, non-linearly","is independent of temperature","rises with temperature","falls linearly with temperature"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A device sleeping at 5 µA that wakes for a long, high-current radio burst is best improved by","options":["cutting the sleep current to 4 µA","using a larger case","reducing the burst duration or frequency","lowering the ADC resolution"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"SPI differs from I²C in that SPI","options":["uses separate chip-select lines and is full duplex","is always slower","uses addresses in the data stream","has no clock"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which statement about CAN bus is true?","options":["it has no error detection","it uses non-destructive arbitration by message ID","it is a wireless standard","it is a single-wire bus"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In the log-distance path-loss model, a larger exponent n means","options":["a longer range","less loss","loss grows faster with distance","a higher frequency"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"LoRa's higher spreading factors give","options":["longer range, lower data rate and longer airtime","better video quality","higher data rate and shorter range","the same range"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which radio technology is best suited to a battery sensor that sends a few bytes per hour over several kilometers?","options":["Bluetooth classic","Ethernet","Wi-Fi","LoRaWAN or NB-IoT"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In MQTT, a retained message is","options":["deleted after delivery","always encrypted","sent twice","stored by the broker and sent to new subscribers of that topic"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The MQTT last-will message is published by the broker when a client","options":["publishes at QoS 2","disconnects unexpectedly","subscribes","changes topic"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"6LoWPAN is needed because","options":["LoRa has no gateways","Wi-Fi is insecure","IPv4 has run out of addresses","IPv6 requires a larger frame than IEEE 802.15.4 supplies, so headers are compressed and packets fragmented"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For a discretized first-order plant, the forward-Euler approximation is accurate when","options":["the plant is unstable","T_s is much smaller than τ","T_s is much larger than τ","the gain is zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Derivative kick on a setpoint change can be avoided by","options":["applying the derivative term to the measurement instead of the error","removing the sensor","lowering the voltage","increasing K_i"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Kalman gain tends to decrease over time for a constant state because","options":["the sensor degrades","the filter is unstable","Q increases","the estimate becomes more certain, so new measurements get less weight"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A digital twin is","options":["a live software model of a physical asset used for monitoring and simulation","a pair of identical firmware images","a backup battery","a duplicate sensor"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"An A/B firmware slot scheme mainly provides","options":["safe rollback if an update fails","double the flash speed","lower power consumption","encryption of data"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A unique per-device credential is preferred to a shared fleet password because","options":["compromising one device does not expose the others","it never expires","it needs no storage","it is shorter"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
